          "id": "variables",
          "title": "Variables",
          "subtitle": "CA453 Unit 2 Concept 16",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Variables: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Variables\n\nA variable is a named memory location used to store a value that can generally change during program execution.\n\n```c\nint age = 21;\n```\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\nVariable\n           │\n           ▼\n      ┌──────────┐\nage → │    21    │\n      └──────────┘\n       Memory\n```\n\nThe value can change:\n\n```c\nage = 22;\n```\n\nNow:\n\n```text\n┌──────────┐\nage → │    22    │\n      └──────────┘\n```\n\nA variable has:\n\n- Name\n- Data type\n- Value\n- Memory location\n- Scope\n- Storage duration characteristics\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nA **variable** is an abstraction of a storage location in memory, identified by a **name** (identifier), associated with a **data type**, and holding a **value** that may be modified during the execution of a program. Formally, a variable binds three things together:\n\n1. **Identifier (Name)** — the symbolic label used in source code to refer to the storage location.\n2. **Type** — determines the kind of value stored and the set of operations permitted on it.\n3. **Storage (Memory Location)** — the physical or logical place where the value resides.\n\nBecause the value is mutable, a variable differs from a **constant**, whose value is fixed after initialization. The statement `int age = 21;` declares a variable named `age` of type `int` and initializes it to `21`. The subsequent assignment `age = 22;` mutates the stored value while the name, type, and memory location remain the same.\n\n---\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe lifecycle of a variable follows a predictable sequence:\n\n1. **Declaration** — the compiler/interpreter is informed of the variable's name and data type.\n2. **Definition / Allocation** — memory is reserved for the variable.\n3. **Initialization** — an initial value is stored (e.g., `int age = 21;`).\n4. **Access / Read** — the value is retrieved using the name.\n5. **Modification / Write** — a new value is assigned (e.g., `age = 22;`).\n6. **Destruction / Deallocation** — memory is released when the variable goes out of scope or the program ends.\n\nConceptually, the name acts as a *handle* to a memory cell:\n\n```text\nName  →  [ Memory Cell ]  →  Value\nage   →  [  0x7FF...  ]   →  21\n```\n\nWhen `age = 22;` executes, only the **contents** of the cell change; the address and the name remain unchanged.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — Value mutation\n\n```c\nint age = 21;   // declaration + initialization\nage = 22;       // modification\n```\n\nState transition:\n\n```text\nBefore:  age → [ 21 ]\nAfter:   age → [ 22 ]\n```\n\n### Example 2 — Multiple variables\n\n```c\nint a = 5;\nint b = 10;\na = a + b;   // a becomes 15\n```\n\n### Example 3 — Variable attributes in action\n\n| Attribute | Example value for `int age = 21;` |\n|-----------|-----------------------------------|\n| Name | `age` |\n| Data type | `int` |\n| Value | `21` (later `22`) |\n| Memory location | an address in memory |\n| Scope | the block in which it is declared |\n| Storage duration | determined by declaration context |\n\n---\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nA variable can be modeled as a mapping from a name to a value over time:\n\n$$\n\\text{value} : \\text{Name} \\times \\text{Time} \\rightarrow \\text{Value}\n$$\n\nFor `age`:\n\n$$\n\\text{value}(\\text{age}, t_0) = 21, \\quad \\text{value}(\\text{age}, t_1) = 22\n$$\n\nAn assignment `x = e` updates the mapping:\n\n$$\n\\text{value}(x, t_{n+1}) = \\text{eval}(e, t_n)\n$$\n\nFor `a = a + b` with `a = 5`, `b = 10`:\n\n$$\na_{new} = a_{old} + b = 5 + 10 = 15\n$$\n\n---\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Variable | Constant |\n|--------|----------|----------|\n| Value change | Can change during execution | Fixed after initialization |\n| Example | `int age = 21; age = 22;` | value cannot be reassigned |\n| Mutability | Mutable | Immutable |\n| Typical use | Counters, accumulators, state | Fixed parameters |\n\n| Attribute | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| Name | Identifier used to reference the variable |\n| Data type | Kind of value stored |\n| Value | Current contents |\n| Memory location | Where the value resides |\n| Scope | Region where the name is visible |\n| Storage duration | Lifetime characteristics of the storage |\n\n---\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing declaration with initialization.** `int age;` declares but does not initialize; `int age = 21;` does both.\n- **Assuming the name changes with the value.** The name and memory location stay fixed; only the value changes.\n- **Forgetting that a variable must have a type** before it can hold a value.\n- **Treating a variable as a constant.** A variable is mutable by definition; if it must not change, a constant is appropriate.\n- **Ignoring scope.** A variable is only accessible within its scope; using it outside leads to errors.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Define a variable as a **named memory location** with a **changeable value**.\n- Always list the **six attributes**: name, data type, value, memory location, scope, storage duration.\n- Use the `age = 21 → 22` example to demonstrate **mutability**.\n- Contrast **variable vs. constant** in a table for quick marks.\n- Remember: assignment changes the **value**, not the **name** or **address**.\n- Draw the box-and-arrow diagram to visualize the name → memory → value relationship.",
