          "id": "initialization-of-variables",
          "title": "Initialization of Variables",
          "subtitle": "CA453 Unit 2 Concept 18",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on Initialization of Variables: definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## Initialization of Variables\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n**Initialization** is the process of assigning an initial (starting) value to a variable at the time it is defined. In C, a variable that is *defined* allocates storage; when a value is supplied in the same statement, the variable is said to be *initialized*. This is distinct from **declaration**, which introduces the variable's name and type to the compiler, and from **assignment**, which stores a value into an already-existing variable.\n\n> **Key distinction:** Initialization happens *once*, at the moment of definition. Assignment can happen *many times* after the variable already exists.\n\n### Working Principle\n\n1. **Declaration** — The compiler is told the variable's type and name. No value is stored.\n2. **Definition** — Storage is reserved for the variable.\n3. **Initialization** — A value is written into that storage as part of the definition.\n4. **Assignment** — A value is written into storage *after* the variable has already been defined.\n\nThe original notes illustrate this progression directly:\n\nDeclaration:\n\n```c\nint age;\n```\n\nInitialization:\n\n```c\nint age = 21;\n```\n\n**Assignment after declaration**\n\n```c\nint age;\nage = 21;\n```\n\nThis is assignment rather than initialization because the variable was already declared.\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\nThe notes provide the canonical initialization examples:\n\n```c\nint age = 21;\nfloat marks = 85.5f;\nchar grade = 'A';\n```\n\nHere each variable is given its starting value in the same statement that defines it:\n\n- `age` is initialized to the integer literal `21`.\n- `marks` is initialized to the floating-point literal `85.5f`.\n- `grade` is initialized to the character literal `'A'`.\n\nContrast the three forms side by side:\n\n```c\n/* 1. Declaration only */\nint age;\n\n/* 2. Initialization (definition + value) */\nint age = 21;\n\n/* 3. Assignment after declaration */\nint age;\nage = 21;\n```\n\nForm (2) is initialization; form (3) is assignment. Both end with `age` holding `21`, but only form (2) initializes at definition time.\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nInitialization can be viewed as a single mapping from a definition to a stored value:\n\n$$\n\\text{initialize}(v, x) : \\quad v \\leftarrow x \\quad \\text{at definition time}\n$$\n\nAssignment, by contrast, is a later update:\n\n$$\n\\text{assign}(v, x) : \\quad v \\leftarrow x \\quad \\text{after } v \\text{ already exists}\n$$\n\nFor a variable initialized once and assigned $n$ times, the total number of value-writes is:\n\n$$\n\\text{writes} = 1 + n\n$$\n\nwhere the $1$ corresponds to the initialization and $n$ to subsequent assignments.\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | Declaration | Initialization | Assignment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Purpose | Introduce name and type | Give starting value at definition | Store value after definition |\n| When it occurs | Before any value exists | At the moment of definition | After the variable already exists |\n| Example | `int age;` | `int age = 21;` | `int age; age = 21;` |\n| Number of times | Once | Once | Can occur many times |\n| Value present after statement | No | Yes | Yes |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Confusing initialization with assignment.** Writing `int age; age = 21;` is *assignment*, not initialization, because the variable was already declared. The notes explicitly flag this: *\"This is assignment rather than initialization because the variable was already declared.\"*\n- **Assuming a declared-but-uninitialized variable holds a value.** A declaration such as `int age;` does not initialize the variable.\n- **Mixing up the literal forms.** Note the suffixes and quoting in the examples: `85.5f` uses the `f` suffix for a float literal, and `'A'` uses single quotes for a character literal.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Be ready to classify a given line of code as **declaration**, **initialization**, or **assignment**. The deciding question is: *did the variable already exist before the value was supplied?*\n- Memorize the three canonical examples exactly as given: `int age = 21;`, `float marks = 85.5f;`, `char grade = 'A';`.\n- Remember the one-line rule: **initialization = value at definition; assignment = value after declaration.**\n- When asked to distinguish the two, use the notes' own example pair — `int age = 21;` (initialization) versus `int age; age = 21;` (assignment) — as your reference answer.",
