          "notes": "## switch Statement\n\nThe switch statement is used for multi-way selection. It compares an expression with several constant case values and executes the matching case.\n\n**Syntax**\n\n```c\nswitch (expression)\n{\n    case constant1:\n        statements;\n        break;\n\n    case constant2:\n        statements;\n        break;\n\n    default:\n        statements;\n}\n```\n\n**Flowchart**\n\n```text\nStart\n                    │\n                    ▼\n               Evaluate\n               expression\n                    │\n          ┌─────────┼─────────┐\n          ▼         ▼         ▼\n       Case 1     Case 2    Case 3\n          │         │         │\n          ▼         ▼         ▼\n       Block 1   Block 2   Block 3\n          │         │         │\n          └─────────┼─────────┘\n                    │\n                    ▼\n                   End\n```\n\n**Example**\n\n```c\nint choice = 2;\n\nswitch (choice)\n{\n    case 1:\n        printf(\"Addition\");\n        break;\n\n    case 2:\n        printf(\"Subtraction\");\n        break;\n\n    case 3:\n        printf(\"Multiplication\");\n        break;\n\n    default:\n        printf(\"Invalid choice\");\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nSubtraction\n```\n\n**break in switch**\n\nbreak terminates the switch statement.\n\nWithout break, execution can continue into the following case statements. This is called fall-through.\n\n```text\ncase 1\n  │\n  ▼\nStatements\n  │\nbreak\n  │\n  ▼\nExit switch\n```\n\n**default**\n\nThe default block executes when none of the case values matches the expression.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nThe **switch statement** is a **multi-way selection (branching) control structure** in the C programming language. It evaluates a single **expression** once and transfers control to the **case label** whose **constant** value equals the value of that expression. If no case constant matches, control is transferred to the optional **default** label. The switch statement is therefore a structured alternative to a long chain of `if–else if–else` comparisons, and it is classified as a **selection statement** in the C language grammar.\n\n## Working Principle\n\n1. The **expression** inside `switch (expression)` is evaluated exactly once.\n2. The resulting value is compared against each **case constant** in the order the cases are written.\n3. When a match is found, control jumps to that case label and the statements following it begin executing.\n4. Execution continues sequentially through subsequent statements until either a **`break`** is encountered or the end of the switch block is reached.\n5. If no case matches, the **`default`** block (if present) executes.\n6. The `break` statement **terminates the switch** and transfers control to the statement immediately following the switch block.\n\nThe evaluation is a **single-pass comparison**, which is why the switch is efficient for multi-way decisions based on one value.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — Matching a case\n\n```c\nint choice = 2;\n\nswitch (choice)\n{\n    case 1:\n        printf(\"Addition\");\n        break;\n\n    case 2:\n        printf(\"Subtraction\");\n        break;\n\n    case 3:\n        printf(\"Multiplication\");\n        break;\n\n    default:\n        printf(\"Invalid choice\");\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\nSubtraction\n```\n\nHere `choice` equals `2`, so control jumps to `case 2` and prints `Subtraction`. The `break` then exits the switch.\n\n### Example 2 — Fall-through (no break)\n\n```text\ncase 1\n  │\n  ▼\nStatements\n  │\nbreak\n  │\n  ▼\nExit switch\n```\n\nWithout a `break`, after executing the statements of a matched case, execution **continues into the following case statements**. This behaviour is called **fall-through**. It is the reason every case in the earlier example ends with a `break`.\n\n### Example 3 — No matching case (default)\n\nIf the expression value does not equal any case constant, the **default** block executes:\n\n```c\ndefault:\n    printf(\"Invalid choice\");\n```\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe switch can be modelled as a **piecewise selection function**. Let the switch expression be $E$ and the case constants be $c_1, c_2, \\dots, c_n$. The executed block is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Block}(E) =\n\\begin{cases}\nB_1, & E = c_1 \\\\\nB_2, & E = c_2 \\\\\n\\vdots & \\vdots \\\\\nB_n, & E = c_n \\\\\nB_{\\text{default}}, & \\text{otherwise}\n\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThe matching condition for case $i$ is the equality predicate:\n\n$$\nE = c_i\n$$\n\nControl transfer occurs at the **first** index $i$ for which $E = c_i$ holds. This is equivalent to a **linear search** over the case constants, evaluated once per switch entry.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Feature | switch Statement | if–else if–else Chain |\n|---|---|---|\n| Selection basis | One expression compared to constant case values | Any boolean condition per branch |\n| Number of evaluations | Expression evaluated once | Each condition evaluated in turn |\n| Case values | Must be **constants** | Can be any expression |\n| Readability for many branches | High (tabular form) | Decreases as branches grow |\n| Fall-through behaviour | Present unless `break` used | Not applicable |\n| Default handling | `default` label | Final `else` |\n| Typical use | Multi-way selection on one value | Range checks, complex conditions |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Omitting `break`** — causes unintended **fall-through** into the next case.\n- **Forgetting the `default`** — leaves no handling for unmatched values.\n- **Using non-constant case values** — case labels must be constants.\n- **Assuming the switch compares ranges** — it only performs equality matching against case constants.\n- **Placing statements before the first case** — such statements are not part of any case block.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Always state that the switch expression is **evaluated once** and compared with **constant case values**.\n- Define **fall-through** clearly: without `break`, execution continues into the following case statements.\n- Remember the role of **`default`**: it executes when **none** of the case values matches the expression.\n- In flowcharts, show the expression evaluated once, branching to Case 1, Case 2, Case 3, and converging to End.\n- Use the piecewise formulation to justify why switch is a **multi-way selection** structure.\n- When writing code answers, include `break` after each case and a `default` block to demonstrate correct usage.",
