          "id": "fgetc",
          "title": "fgetc()",
          "subtitle": "CA453 Unit 5 Concept 32",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on fgetc(): definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## fgetc()\n\nfgetc() reads one character from a file.\n\nSyntax:\n\n```c\nint fgetc(FILE *stream);\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```c\nint ch;\n\nch = fgetc(fp);\n```\n\nReading an entire file:\n\n```c\nint ch;\n\nwhile ((ch = fgetc(fp)) != EOF)\n{\n    putchar(ch);\n}\n```\n\nDiagram:\n\n```text\nFile\n┌─────────────────┐\n│ H e l l o       │\n└─────────────────┘\n       │\n       ▼\n    fgetc()\n       │\n       ▼\n      'H'\n```\n\nThe return type is int so that all unsigned-char values plus the special EOF value can be represented.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n**fgetc()** is a standard C library function declared in `<stdio.h>` that reads a **single character** from a specified input stream (typically a file opened via `fopen()`). It is a **character-oriented input function** that advances the file position indicator by one byte after each successful read. The function returns the character read as an `unsigned char` value cast to `int`, or the special macro `EOF` (End Of File) if a read error occurs or the end of the file is reached.\n\nFormally:\n\n- **Header:** `<stdio.h>`\n- **Prototype:** `int fgetc(FILE *stream);`\n- **Return value:** On success, returns the next character as an `int`. On failure or end-of-file, returns `EOF`.\n\n## Working Principle\n\n1. **Stream Access:** `fgetc()` receives a `FILE *` pointer that references an open stream (file, stdin, etc.).\n2. **Buffer Interaction:** The C runtime maintains an internal buffer for the stream. `fgetc()` retrieves the next character from this buffer. If the buffer is empty, the library performs an underlying read from the operating system.\n3. **Position Advance:** After a successful read, the file position indicator moves forward by one byte.\n4. **Return Mechanism:** The character is returned as an `int`. This widening is deliberate: it allows the function to return all possible byte values (0–255 for `unsigned char`) **plus** a distinct out-of-band value `EOF` (typically -1) to signal end-of-file or error.\n5. **EOF Handling:** When the end of the file is encountered, `fgetc()` returns `EOF`. Subsequent calls may continue to return `EOF` until the stream state is cleared (e.g., via `clearerr()`).\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Reading a Single Character\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void)\n{\n    FILE *fp = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\n    if (fp == NULL) return 1;\n\n    int ch = fgetc(fp);   // read first character\n    if (ch != EOF)\n        putchar(ch);\n\n    fclose(fp);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Example 2: Reading an Entire File Character by Character\n\n```c\nint ch;\n\nwhile ((ch = fgetc(fp)) != EOF)\n{\n    putchar(ch);\n}\n```\n\nThis loop reads every character until `EOF` is returned. The assignment inside the condition is a common C idiom.\n\n### Example 3: Counting Characters in a File\n\n```c\nint ch;\nlong count = 0;\n\nwhile ((ch = fgetc(fp)) != EOF)\n{\n    count++;\n}\nprintf(\"Total characters: %ld\\n\", count);\n```\n\n### Example 4: Reading from Standard Input\n\n```c\nint ch;\nwhile ((ch = fgetc(stdin)) != EOF)\n{\n    putchar(ch);\n}\n```\n\n`fgetc()` works on any `FILE *` stream, including `stdin`.\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet the file be a sequence of bytes \\( B = b_0, b_1, b_2, \\dots, b_{n-1} \\), where each \\( b_i \\in \\{0, 1, \\dots, 255\\} \\).\n\nDefine the file position indicator \\( p \\), initially \\( p = 0 \\).\n\nThe behaviour of `fgetc()` can be modelled as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{fgetc}(stream) =\n\\begin{cases}\nb_p, & \\text{if } p < n \\\\\n\\text{EOF}, & \\text{if } p \\ge n \\text{ or error}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nAfter a successful read:\n\n\\[\np \\leftarrow p + 1\n\\]\n\nThe return value is of type `int`, which can represent:\n\n\\[\n\\{0, 1, \\dots, 255\\} \\cup \\{\\text{EOF}\\}\n\\]\n\nSince `EOF` is typically \\(-1\\), it lies outside the range of `unsigned char`, ensuring no ambiguity between a valid byte and the end-of-file marker.\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Feature | `fgetc()` | `getc()` | `getchar()` | `fgets()` |\n|---------|-----------|----------|-------------|-----------|\n| Reads | One character | One character | One character from `stdin` | A line (string) |\n| Prototype | `int fgetc(FILE *stream);` | `int getc(FILE *stream);` | `int getchar(void);` | `char *fgets(char *s, int n, FILE *stream);` |\n| Stream | Any `FILE *` | Any `FILE *` | Always `stdin` | Any `FILE *` |\n| Implementation | Function | Often a macro | Macro/function | Function |\n| Return type | `int` | `int` | `int` | `char *` |\n| EOF handling | Returns `EOF` | Returns `EOF` | Returns `EOF` | Returns `NULL` on error/EOF |\n| Typical use | File reading | File reading (faster) | Console input | Line-based input |\n\n**Key distinction:** `fgetc()` is a true function, whereas `getc()` is often implemented as a macro for speed. `getchar()` is equivalent to `getc(stdin)` / `fgetc(stdin)`.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Using `char` instead of `int` for the return value.**\n   ```c\n   char ch = fgetc(fp);   // WRONG\n   ```\n   If `char` is signed, a valid byte like `0xFF` may be misinterpreted as `EOF`. Always use `int`.\n\n2. **Forgetting to check for `EOF`.** Reading past the end of a file without checking leads to undefined behaviour or infinite loops.\n\n3. **Not checking if `fopen()` succeeded.** Passing a `NULL` `FILE *` to `fgetc()` is undefined behaviour.\n\n4. **Confusing `EOF` with a character.** `EOF` is not a character stored in the file; it is a sentinel value returned by the function.\n\n5. **Assuming `fgetc()` returns `char`.** The return type is `int` precisely to accommodate `EOF`.\n\n6. **Ignoring read errors.** `fgetc()` returns `EOF` on both end-of-file and error. Use `feof()` and `ferror()` to distinguish.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- **Memorise the prototype:** `int fgetc(FILE *stream);` — the `int` return type is a frequent exam question.\n- **Explain why the return type is `int`:** To represent all 256 possible `unsigned char` values plus the distinct `EOF` value.\n- **Know the idiom:** `while ((ch = fgetc(fp)) != EOF)` — parentheses around the assignment are essential.\n- **Distinguish `fgetc()` vs `getc()` vs `getchar()`:** `fgetc` is a function; `getc` is often a macro; `getchar` reads from `stdin` only.\n- **Remember the header:** `<stdio.h>`.\n- **Be ready to trace a loop:** Given a file \"Hello\", show what `fgetc()` returns on each call: `'H'`, `'e'`, `'l'`, `'l'`, `'o'`, then `EOF`.\n- **Link to file position:** Each successful call advances the file pointer by one byte.\n- **Error vs EOF:** Mention `feof()` and `ferror()` for robust code.",
