          "id": "fwrite",
          "title": "fwrite()",
          "subtitle": "CA453 Unit 5 Concept 40",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on fwrite(): definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## fwrite()\n\n`fwrite()` writes blocks of binary data to a file. It is the standard C library function used for binary output, allowing structured data (such as structs, arrays, or raw memory buffers) to be written directly to a file stream.\n\n### Academic Definition\n\n`fwrite()` is a standard input/output library function declared in `<stdio.h>` that performs **unformatted binary output**. It transfers a specified number of fixed-size data items from a memory buffer to a file stream. Unlike text-oriented functions such as `fprintf()`, `fwrite()` does not translate, format, or interpret the data — it copies the raw byte representation of the memory block directly into the file. This makes it the canonical function for persisting structures, arrays, and records in binary files.\n\n### Syntax\n\n```c\nfwrite(pointer, size, count, fp);\n```\n\nWhere:\n\n- **`pointer`** — address of the memory block containing the data to be written (e.g., `&s` or an array name).\n- **`size`** — size in bytes of each individual element to write (commonly `sizeof(...)`).\n- **`count`** — number of elements to write.\n- **`fp`** — file pointer to an open file, opened in a binary write/update mode.\n\n### Working Principle\n\n```text\nStructure\n    │\n    ▼\n  fwrite()\n    │\n    ▼\nBinary File\n```\n\n1. The program prepares a block of memory (for example, a `struct Student` variable).\n2. `fwrite()` is called with the address of that block, the size of one element, and the number of elements.\n3. The function copies `size × count` bytes from the memory buffer into the file stream associated with `fp`.\n4. The bytes are stored exactly as they exist in memory — no conversion, no formatting, no text encoding.\n5. The file position indicator advances by the number of bytes written.\n\n### Detailed Examples\n\n**Example — writing a single structure:**\n\n```c\nstruct Student s = {101, 85.5};\n\nfwrite(&s, sizeof(s), 1, fp);\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `&s` is the address of the structure in memory.\n- `sizeof(s)` is the size of one complete `Student` record in bytes.\n- `1` indicates that exactly one such record is written.\n- `fp` must be a file pointer opened for binary writing.\n\nThe entire structure — including the integer `101` and the floating-point value `85.5` — is written as a contiguous block of raw bytes.\n\n**Example — writing an array of records:**\n\n```c\nstruct Student arr[10];\nfwrite(arr, sizeof(struct Student), 10, fp);\n```\n\nThis writes all ten records in a single call, since `arr` decays to a pointer to its first element.\n\n### Mathematical Formulations\n\nThe total number of bytes transferred by a single `fwrite()` call is:\n\n$$\n\\text{bytes written} = \\text{size} \\times \\text{count}\n$$\n\nThe return value of `fwrite()` is the number of **elements** successfully written:\n\n$$\n\\text{return value} = \\text{count} \\quad \\text{(on full success)}\n$$\n\nA short count indicates a partial write or error:\n\n$$\n\\text{return value} < \\text{count} \\implies \\text{write error or end-of-medium condition}\n$$\n\n### Comparative Analysis\n\n| Aspect | `fwrite()` | `fprintf()` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Data form | Binary (raw bytes) | Text (formatted characters) |\n| Formatting | None | Format specifiers (`%d`, `%f`, etc.) |\n| Typical use | Structs, arrays, records | Human-readable output |\n| Portability of file | Machine/representation dependent | Text, generally portable |\n| Read counterpart | `fread()` | `fscanf()` |\n\n| Aspect | `fwrite()` | `fputc()` / `fputs()` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Unit written | Block of `size × count` bytes | Single character / string |\n| Granularity | Record-level | Character-level |\n| Efficiency | High for bulk data | Lower for bulk data |\n\n### Common Mistakes\n\n- **Forgetting to open the file in binary mode.** Binary data should be written to a file opened in a binary mode; text mode may perform newline translation.\n- **Passing the structure instead of its address.** `fwrite(s, ...)` is incorrect; the address `&s` (or an array name) is required.\n- **Mismatched `size` and `count`.** Using the wrong `sizeof` or count writes the wrong number of bytes.\n- **Ignoring the return value.** A short return signals that not all elements were written.\n- **Assuming the file is human-readable.** Binary files produced by `fwrite()` contain raw bytes and are not meant to be read as text.\n\n### Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the argument order: **pointer, size, count, fp**.\n- The return value is the **number of elements** written, not the number of bytes.\n- `fwrite()` is the write counterpart of `fread()`; they are typically used as a pair for record-based file handling.\n- Use `sizeof(s)` for the size argument when writing a single structure.\n- State clearly that `fwrite()` performs **unformatted binary output** — this distinguishes it from `fprintf()`.\n- The classic diagram to reproduce:\n\n```text\nStructure\n    │\n    ▼\n  fwrite()\n    │\n    ▼\nBinary File\n```",
