          "notes": "## File Position Indicator\n\nEvery opened stream maintains a current file position.\n\n```text\nFile:\n┌────┬────┬────┬────┬────┬────┐\n│ A  │ B  │ C  │ D  │ E  │ F  │\n└────┴────┴────┴────┴────┴────┘\n             ▲\n             │\n        File Position\n```\n\nFunctions such as:\n\n```text\nfgetc()\nfputc()\nfseek()\nrewind()\n```\n\ncan affect or use the file position.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\nThe **file position indicator** is an internal attribute of an open stream that records the offset (in bytes) of the **next** byte to be read from or written to the associated file. It is a per-stream state variable maintained by the C standard library, not a property of the file itself. Each call to an input or output function advances this indicator automatically; positioning functions such as `fseek()` and `rewind()` set it explicitly. The indicator is meaningful only while the stream is open — it is created when the stream is opened and discarded when the stream is closed.\n\n## Working Principle\n\n1. **Stream creation** — When a file is opened (e.g., via `fopen()`), the library allocates a `FILE` object and initializes its file position indicator, typically to offset `0` (the beginning of the file).\n2. **Sequential access** — Each read (`fgetc()`, `fgets()`, `fread()`) or write (`fputc()`, `fputs()`, `fwrite()`) operation consumes or produces bytes and advances the indicator by the number of bytes transferred.\n3. **Explicit positioning** — `fseek()` moves the indicator to a specified offset relative to a reference point (`SEEK_SET`, `SEEK_CUR`, `SEEK_END`). `rewind()` resets it to the beginning of the file.\n4. **Querying** — `ftell()` returns the current value of the indicator as a `long` offset from the start of the file.\n5. **Persistence across operations** — The indicator persists between function calls on the same stream, which is why successive `fgetc()` calls return consecutive characters.\n\n```text\nInitial state (after fopen):\nFile:  [ A ][ B ][ C ][ D ][ E ][ F ]\n        ▲\n        position = 0\n\nAfter one fgetc() returns 'A':\nFile:  [ A ][ B ][ C ][ D ][ E ][ F ]\n             ▲\n             position = 1\n\nAfter fseek(stream, 3, SEEK_SET):\nFile:  [ A ][ B ][ C ][ D ][ E ][ F ]\n                       ▲\n                       position = 3\n```\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1 — Sequential read advances the indicator\n\n```c\nFILE *fp = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nint c1 = fgetc(fp);   /* reads 'A', position -> 1 */\nint c2 = fgetc(fp);   /* reads 'B', position -> 2 */\nlong pos = ftell(fp); /* pos == 2 */\nfclose(fp);\n```\n\n### Example 2 — Repositioning with fseek\n\n```c\nFILE *fp = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nfseek(fp, 3, SEEK_SET);  /* position -> 3 */\nint c = fgetc(fp);       /* reads 'D', position -> 4 */\nfclose(fp);\n```\n\n### Example 3 — Rewinding to the start\n\n```c\nFILE *fp = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nfgetc(fp);               /* position -> 1 */\nfgetc(fp);               /* position -> 2 */\nrewind(fp);              /* position -> 0 */\nint c = fgetc(fp);       /* reads 'A' again */\nfclose(fp);\n```\n\n### Example 4 — Writing also uses the indicator\n\n```c\nFILE *fp = fopen(\"out.txt\", \"w\");\nfputc('X', fp);          /* writes 'X', position -> 1 */\nfputc('Y', fp);          /* writes 'Y', position -> 2 */\nfclose(fp);\n```\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet \\(p\\) denote the current file position (offset in bytes from the start of the file), and let \\(n\\) be the number of bytes transferred by an operation.\n\n- **Sequential read/write of \\(n\\) bytes:**\n  \\[\n  p_{\\text{new}} = p_{\\text{old}} + n\n  \\]\n\n- **`fseek(stream, offset, whence)`:**\n\n  \\[\n  p_{\\text{new}} =\n  \\begin{cases}\n  \\text{offset} & \\text{if whence} = \\texttt{SEEK\\_SET} \\\\\n  p_{\\text{old}} + \\text{offset} & \\text{if whence} = \\texttt{SEEK\\_CUR} \\\\\n  L + \\text{offset} & \\text{if whence} = \\texttt{SEEK\\_END}\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n  where \\(L\\) is the total length of the file in bytes.\n\n- **`rewind(stream)`** is equivalent to:\n  \\[\n  p_{\\text{new}} = 0\n  \\]\n\n- **`ftell(stream)`** returns the current value of \\(p\\).\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Function   | Direction | Effect on File Position Indicator                  | Typical Use                     |\n|------------|-----------|----------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------|\n| `fgetc()`  | Input     | Advances by 1 byte on success                      | Read one character              |\n| `fputc()`  | Output    | Advances by 1 byte on success                      | Write one character             |\n| `fseek()`  | Both      | Sets position to a computed offset                 | Random access                   |\n| `rewind()` | Both      | Resets position to 0                                | Restart reading from beginning  |\n| `ftell()`  | Both      | Does **not** change position; reports it           | Query current offset            |\n\n| Reference Point | Symbol       | Meaning of `offset` argument              |\n|-----------------|--------------|-------------------------------------------|\n| Start of file   | `SEEK_SET`   | Absolute byte offset from byte 0          |\n| Current position| `SEEK_CUR`   | Relative displacement from current \\(p\\)  |\n| End of file     | `SEEK_END`   | Displacement relative to end of file      |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n- **Assuming the indicator is shared between streams.** Each open stream has its own independent file position indicator, even if two streams refer to the same file.\n- **Forgetting that `ftell()` does not move the indicator.** It only reports the current offset.\n- **Confusing `rewind()` with `fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_SET)`.** They are functionally similar for positioning, but `rewind()` also clears the stream's error indicator.\n- **Mixing reads and writes without an intervening positioning call.** In update modes (`\"r+\"`, `\"w+\"`, `\"a+\"`), the standard requires a positioning call (`fseek`, `rewind`, or `fflush`) between a read and a write (or vice versa).\n- **Ignoring the return value of `fseek()`.** A nonzero return indicates failure, and the indicator may be left in an unspecified state.\n- **Using `fseek()` on non-seekable streams** (e.g., pipes, terminals), where positioning is not supported.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the **three `SEEK_*` constants** and how each interprets the `offset` argument — this is a frequent short-answer question.\n- Be able to **trace the value of `ftell()`** after a sequence of `fgetc()`, `fseek()`, and `rewind()` calls; draw the file as a row of boxes with an arrow, as shown above.\n- Know that **`fgetc()` and `fputc()` advance the indicator by exactly one byte** on success.\n- Distinguish **`rewind()`** (resets to 0, clears error flag) from **`fseek()`** (general repositioning).\n- For update-mode streams, cite the rule: **a positioning call is required between read and write operations**.\n- Expect questions that combine `fopen`, `fgetc`/`fputc`, `fseek`, `ftell`, and `rewind` in a single code snippet — practice tracing the indicator step by step.",
