          "id": "ftell",
          "title": "ftell()",
          "subtitle": "CA453 Unit 5 Concept 42",
          "summary": "Complete study notes on ftell(): definitions, principles, worked examples, formulas, and exam-relevant details derived from the syllabus source.",
          "estimatedMinutes": 8,
          "notes": "## ftell()\n\n`ftell()` returns the current file position indicator.\n\nSyntax:\n\n```c\nlong ftell(FILE *fp);\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```c\nlong position;\n\nposition = ftell(fp);\n```\n\nConcept:\n\n```text\nA B C D E F\n      ▲\n      │\n   Position = 3\n```\n\nThe exact interpretation of positions depends on the stream and mode, especially for text streams.\n\n---\n\n## Academic Definition\n\n`ftell()` is a standard C library function declared in `<stdio.h>` that returns the current value of the **file position indicator** for the specified stream. The file position indicator represents the offset, in bytes, from the beginning of the file to the location where the next read or write operation will occur. The function returns a value of type `long`. If an error occurs, `ftell()` returns `-1L` and sets the error indicator `errno` to a system-specific positive value.\n\nFormally, `ftell()` is used in conjunction with `fseek()` and `rewind()` to manage random access within a file. It allows a program to record a position, perform other operations, and later return to that exact position.\n\n## Working Principle\n\nThe file position indicator is an internal attribute of every `FILE` stream. It is advanced automatically by read and write operations:\n\n- After reading `n` bytes, the indicator advances by `n` bytes.\n- After writing `n` bytes, the indicator advances by `n` bytes.\n\n`ftell()` simply queries this internal counter and returns it as a `long`. The value is meaningful only for **seekable streams** — typically disk files opened in binary mode. For non-seekable streams (pipes, terminals, sockets), the behavior is undefined or the function may fail.\n\nThe conceptual diagram illustrates the idea:\n\n```text\nA B C D E F\n      ▲\n      │\n   Position = 3\n```\n\nHere, the file contains six bytes (`A` through `F`). The position indicator points to the byte at offset `3` (the fourth byte, `D`), meaning the next read will return `D`.\n\n## Detailed Examples\n\n### Example 1: Recording and Restoring a Position\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    FILE *fp = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"rb\");\n    if (!fp) return 1;\n\n    long pos;\n\n    fgetc(fp);              /* read 'A' */\n    fgetc(fp);              /* read 'B' */\n    pos = ftell(fp);        /* pos = 2 */\n\n    fgetc(fp);              /* read 'C' */\n    fgetc(fp);              /* read 'D' */\n\n    fseek(fp, pos, SEEK_SET);  /* return to offset 2 */\n    int c = fgetc(fp);         /* c = 'C' again */\n\n    fclose(fp);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Example 2: Determining File Size\n\nA common idiom combines `fseek()` and `ftell()` to compute the size of a binary file:\n\n```c\nfseek(fp, 0L, SEEK_END);   /* move to end */\nlong size = ftell(fp);     /* size in bytes */\nrewind(fp);                /* return to start */\n```\n\n### Example 3: Tracking Progress\n\n```c\nlong start = ftell(fp);\n/* ... perform reads ... */\nlong consumed = ftell(fp) - start;\nprintf(\"Bytes consumed: %ld\\n\", consumed);\n```\n\n## Mathematical Formulations\n\nLet the file be a sequence of bytes indexed from `0` to `N-1`. If the position indicator is at offset `p`, then:\n\n- The next byte read corresponds to index `p`.\n- After reading one byte, the new position is `p + 1`.\n- After reading `k` bytes, the new position is `p + k`.\n\nThe relationship between `ftell()` and `fseek()` can be expressed as:\n\n$$\n\\text{ftell}(fp) = p \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{fseek}(fp,\\ p,\\ \\text{SEEK\\_SET}) = 0\n$$\n\nThat is, seeking to the value returned by `ftell()` restores the stream to the same logical position.\n\nFor a file of size `N` bytes, after seeking to the end:\n\n$$\n\\text{ftell}(fp) = N\n$$\n\n## Comparative Analysis\n\n| Function | Purpose | Return Type | Typical Use |\n|----------|---------|-------------|-------------|\n| `ftell()` | Get current position | `long` | Save position, compute size |\n| `fseek()` | Set position | `int` | Jump to a known offset |\n| `rewind()` | Reset to start | `void` | Return to beginning |\n| `fgetpos()` | Get position (portable) | `int` | Large files, text streams |\n| `fsetpos()` | Set position (portable) | `int` | Restore position from `fgetpos()` |\n\n`ftell()` is simpler but less portable for very large files or text streams than `fgetpos()`/`fsetpos()`, which use the opaque `fpos_t` type.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n1. **Assuming byte offsets on text streams.** On text streams, the value returned by `ftell()` may not be a simple byte count because of newline translation or other platform-specific transformations. The C standard only guarantees that the value can be passed to `fseek()` to restore the position.\n\n2. **Ignoring the `-1L` return value.** Failing to check for errors can lead to silent bugs, especially on non-seekable streams.\n\n3. **Using `ftell()` on a stream after an error.** The position indicator may be indeterminate.\n\n4. **Mixing `ftell()` with `fgetpos()`.** These use different position representations; do not interchange them.\n\n5. **Assuming `long` is always 64-bit.** On some platforms `long` is 32 bits, limiting file sizes to 2 GB. Use `fgetpos()`/`fsetpos()` for larger files.\n\n## Exam Tips\n\n- Remember the **return type is `long`**, not `int`.\n- The error return value is **`-1L`**.\n- `ftell()` is declared in **`<stdio.h>`**.\n- The classic file-size idiom is: `fseek(fp, 0, SEEK_END); size = ftell(fp); rewind(fp);`\n- For **text streams**, `ftell()` values are only meaningful when passed back to `fseek()`.\n- Distinguish `ftell()` (returns position) from `fseek()` (sets position) and `rewind()` (resets to start).\n- Be prepared to explain why `fgetpos()`/`fsetpos()` are preferred for portability and large files.",
